  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 120 AM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nThe last of evening/late night showers and thunderstorms were moving \nacross southwest Kansas along roughly Ness City to Dodge City to \nMeade line shortly before 06Z. The remnant shower/thunderstorm \nactivity will eventually dissolve all together overnight, and the \nfocus will shift more toward the strong cold front later today.\n\nOn the midnight CDT analysis, observations were showing northerly \nwinds approaching the far northwest corner of northwestern Kansas, \nbut the noticeably cooler air was still up in northwestern Nebraska \nin the Sandhills where temperatures had fallen into the lower to mid \n50s (51F at Chadron, NE). As the vigorous storm system up north \ncontinues to pull away from the Northern Plains, the low level cold \nair advection will increase and expand quickly south today. \nTemperatures, low cloud cover, and precipitation chances will all be \nchallenges in the forecast today with the first true shot of Autumn \nair into southwestern Kansas. Given the magnitude of cooler air up \nnorth and latest models coming into better alignment, we have \ntrended cooler with the official forecast -- going lower than the \nbaseline NBM, incorporating some CONSShort weight which has a better \nhandle on the upstream temperatures and better representation of \nhourly temperature trends with the cold air advection and low cloud \ncover preventing a normal diurnal temperature curve. Expect \ntemperatures to hold steady or fall during the afternoon with most \nareas in the 60s to lower 70s by late afternoon north of a Hugoton \nto Dodge City Kinsley line. \n\nEven though the surface front will be pushing south, the frontal \nzone around 5 kft will remain quasi-stationary across southwest \nKansas with respectable dewpoints of +14 to +16C at that ~5 kft \nlevel (850mb). Southwest Kansas will also be well within the \nnorthwestern periphery of a still fairly coherent North American \nMonsoon circulation which as not been disrupted as of yet in this \npattern (mainly thanks to a persistent subtropical high of 597 \ndecameters at 500mb centered over the southeastern CONUS. POPs will \nremain fairly high as a result through Tuesday Night.",
